{"answers":[{"question_id":"Q006_ubiquitin_like_function_divergence","answer":"**Relationship SCOPe supports.** In the current release, SCOPe 2.08 (frozen 2021-07-28, latest periodic update 2.08-2026-08-16), ubiquitin-related proteins, UBX domains, and Ras-binding domains (RBDs) are three sibling *families* within one *superfamily*, d.15.1 "Ubiquitin-like" (sunid 54236): d.15.1.1 "Ubiquitin-related" (sunid 54237; ubiquitin, SUMO-1/2/3, Nedd8, Rub1, Elongin B, ISG15, Rad23/Hhr23 UBL domains, parkin UBL, etc.), d.15.1.2 "UBX domain" (sunid 54250; p47, Faf1, UBXD6, TUG, KIAA0794), and d.15.1.5 "Ras-binding domain, RBD" (sunid 54263; c-Raf1 and A-Raf RBDs, RalGDS/Rgl/Rlf RA domains, PI3K RBD, Byr2, afadin, PLC-epsilon, RGS14, RapGEF5, RASSF8, GRB-7). The superfamily sits in fold d.15 "beta-Grasp (ubiquitin-like)" (sunid 54235), class d (alpha+beta). The superfamily also holds other families (GABARAP-like d.15.1.3, first domain of FERM d.15.1.4, BM-002-like d.15.1.6, APG12-like d.15.1.7, DIX domain d.15.1.8, viral protease UBL d.15.1.9, Rho GTPase-binding domain d.15.1.10, plus automated matches d.15.1.0).

The evolutionary strength of each level matters: (1) *Family* membership (e.g. within d.15.1.1) implies clear common ancestry with detectable sequence similarity, so function and the ubiquitin/UBL-specific features (C-terminal Gly-Gly conjugation tail, Ile44 hydrophobic patch recognised by UBDs, Lys48/Lys63 chain linkages) are reasonably transferable among ubiquitin, Nedd8, SUMO and UBL domains, with care. (2) *Superfamily* membership (d.15.1) is SCOPe's assertion of probable common evolutionary origin based on shared structure and sometimes shared functional features despite low sequence identity: ubiquitin-related, UBX and RBD domains are therefore homologous *domains* sharing the beta-grasp architecture, but they have diverged into different functional roles: ubiquitin/UBLs are covalent modifiers; UBX domains are non-covalent p97/VCP (Cdc48) N-domain adaptor modules that lack the Gly-Gly tail and are never conjugated; RBDs/RA domains are non-covalent effector modules that bind the switch regions of Ras-family GTPases via an intermolecular beta-sheet. The biologically useful conclusion is that all three are a single ancient protein-protein-interaction scaffold, repeatedly recruited as a recognition module; this explains why many signalling proteins (Raf, PI3K, RalGDS, afadin) and p97 cofactors carry a "ubiquitin-like" domain that is not ubiquitin at all, and it justifies using ubiquitin as a structural template for modelling UBX or RBD domains. (3) *Fold*-level only relatives (other d.15 superfamilies such as d.15.2 CAD & PB1 domains, d.15.3 MoaD/ThiS, d.15.4 2Fe-2S ferredoxin-like, d.15.6 superantigen toxin C-terminal domains, d.15.7 Ig-binding domains, d.15.10 TGS-like) share only the beta-grasp topology; SCOPe does not assert homology at the fold level (MoaD/ThiS is a well-known case where a ubiquitin-like conjugation chemistry exists but SCOP keeps it as a separate superfamily), so no functional or evolutionary inference should be drawn from a shared fold alone.

**Unsafe annotation transfer.** It would be unsafe to transfer ubiquitin's molecular function (covalent conjugation by E1/E2/E3 enzymes, Gly-Gly isopeptide linkage, polyubiquitin chain formation, proteasome targeting, deubiquitinase substrate) to UBX domains or RBDs merely because they share superfamily d.15.1; UBX and RBD proteins are not post-translational modifiers. Equally, Ras/Rap-binding (GTPase effector) function must not be transferred from d.15.1.5 to d.15.1.1 or d.15.1.2 members, and p97/VCP-binding must not be transferred from UBX to the others. Even within the RBD family, the actual GTPase partner and affinity (H-Ras vs Rap1 vs Ral) differ between Raf RBD, RalGDS RA, and PI3K RBD, so partner identity requires experimental evidence (binding/structural data of the complex), not family membership. Likewise within d.15.1.1, SUMO, Nedd8 and ISG15 use distinct E1/E2 cascades and interaction surfaces, so pathway-specific annotation should not be copied from ubiquitin. Transfer of binding-site residues (e.g. ubiquitin Ile44 patch, or the RBD beta2/alpha1 Ras-contact residues) across families is not justified because surface residues are not conserved at superfamily-level divergence. Also note that membership in d.15.1.0 "automated matches" indicates assignment by sequence/structure matching rather than manual curation, and some fold-level members such as Rho GTPase-binding domains (d.15.1.10) and Ras-binding domains illustrate that "GTPase-binding" arose convergently/independently as a use of the scaffold, so function should be verified by external evidence (biochemical binding assays, co-crystal/NMR structures of complexes, conjugation assays, Gly-Gly motif and E1-recognition sequence signatures, genetic data) before annotation.","evidence_used":["/api/v1","/api/v1/releases","/api/v1/releases/2.08","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sccs/d.15","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sccs/d.15.1","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/54236","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/54236/children","/api/v1/rel